# A `&pty` job gets a real pseudo-terminal instead of plain pipes --
# stdout/stderr merge onto one stream, stdin reaches a real line
# discipline (Ctrl-D means EOF, Ctrl-C/Ctrl-Z become real signals), and
# `ctl resize` actually works. Everything below is scriptable and runs
# to completion just like every other example here -- attach(), the
# actual interactive client, is the one exception (see the very bottom
# of this file for why, and the one line to try it yourself).

# `pty` written directly after `&`, no separator required -- same
# shape as a plain `&` job otherwise. This one waits for one line of
# stdin, then reports its own terminal size and echoes back what it
# read -- everything a real interactive program might do.
j := %sh "-c" "read x; stty size; echo got $x" &pty
had_pty := j.status.pty

# `ctl resize ROWS COLS` -- matches `stty size`'s own output order --
# actually changes what the job's own pty reports, since it's a real
# terminal now, not a pipe. A plain (non-pty) job's resize only ever
# explains that there's no terminal to resize.
j.ctl = "resize 40 120"

# A &pty job's stdin field is writable, unlike a plain job's (which
# pre-closes its stdin immediately, since nothing else would ever
# close it) -- this reaches the job exactly like typing at a real
# terminal would.
j.stdin = "hello from a script\n"

j | wait
output := j.stdout

format(
    "job opted into a pty: {}; after resize+stdin scripting, its output was: {}",
    had_pty,
    output,
)

# --- Trying attach() yourself (not run by this script) ---
#
# attach(job) is the actual interactive client: it takes over your
# terminal (or, inside the TUI, the whole screen with a real
# terminal-emulator widget) and streams bytes directly to/from the
# job's pty -- Ctrl-D/Ctrl-C/Ctrl-Z behave like a real terminal, window
# size follows yours, and Ctrl-] detaches back to the prompt without
# touching the job (typed twice, sends a literal Ctrl-] through
# instead). It blocks until you detach or the job exits, which is
# exactly why this script -- meant to run to completion on its own and
# print one final summary, like every other example here -- doesn't
# call it.
#
# To see it for real, open `9sh` (or `9sh -repl`) and paste:
#
#   j := %sh &pty
#   attach(j)
#
# You'll land in a real interactive shell running inside that job's
# pty. Ctrl-] gets you back.
